<HTML><TITLE>SAVOY : Bio</TITLE><CENTER><P><BODY background="IMAGE/bkgr3.jpg" text=bbbb00 link=bbbb00 vlink=aa5500><IMG SRC="image/toptitle.jpg"><BR><IMG SRC="IMAGE/line big.JPG" border=0><BR></CENTER><font size=5><BR><CENTER><FONT SIZE=5>	paul savoy<br><FONT SIZE=2>guitar, vocals <br><FONT SIZE=5>	lauren savoy<br><FONT SIZE=2> guitar, vocals<br><FONT SIZE=5>	frode unneland <br><FONT SIZE=2>drums<br><FONT SIZE=5>	greg calvert <br><FONT SIZE=2>bass<br><P><IMG SRC="image/line sm.jpg"></CENTER><TABLE CELLSPACING=0 CELLPADDING=0><TR><TD><CENTER><IMG SRC="image/savlong.jpg" align=middle><br></CENTER><TD><PRE></PRE><FONT SIZE=4>Consider guitarist/singer <B>Paul Savoy</B> is holed up for an Arctic winter in his Oslo basement, all alone with  his songwriter/film maker wife Lauren and 800 square feet  of recording equipment to keep him warm.  What else can a world-class performer do but make the album of his life?  That's the engaging scenario behind <B>MARY IS COMING </B>the gallant debut WarnerBros. album from <B>SAVOY.</B><BR><BR>Known planetwide as a founding member of <FONT SIZE=5><B>a-ha</B><FONT SIZE=4>, Paul had no iron-clad objectives when he and Lauren set out to write and record <B>MARY IS COMING</B> other than to have a good time making music.   In fact, the album emerged incrementally ain all its organic splendor.  "In the past,  a lot of time would be spent going through sound after sound," Paul says.  " It always became a chore. Here the idea was to choose a few favorite sounds and go from there."   An embarrassment of shortages?  Hardly. Though undeniably fat-free, Savoy's music revels in taut muscularity,  highlighted by Paul's virtouso guitar playing and plaitive vocals.<BR><BR>Written, produced and engineered completely by Paul and Lauren, <B>MARY IS COMING</B> is indeed a marked departure from Paul's previous recorded work.  Paul himself played guitar and bass, with drummer Frode Unerland an Lauren's guitar and background providing the only accompaniment.  </TR></TABLE><FONT SIZE=4><P>The albums rough-hewn sound was deliberete. " I tend to write very melodically," notes Paul,"and so in the arrangements I try to avoid too much sugar on top.  The melodic elements come out much more when they have something to hit against.    Adds Lauren, " If we didn't get it in two takes, we moved on.  I like atrashy feel."<BR><BR><TABLE CELLSPACING=0 CELLPADDING=0><TR><TD><FONT SIZE=4>Trashy, you say?  From the opening notes of the corrosive lead track <B>"Daylight's Wasting"</B>, MARY IS COMING offers its own coarse version of salvation.  Songs like the smoldering<B>"Foolish," "Tears From A Stone," </B>the harmonically rich <B>"We Will Never Forget,"</B>and the fuzz-filtered salute to self -sacrifice <B>"Get Up Now,"</B> exemplify Savoy's serrated guitar-centered approach.On the other hand, the fragile ballad <B>"Velvet," "1/2 Hours Worth," </B>and the simple elegy" Mary is coming," all display Savoy's ample Soul ... of the Rubber variety.<BR><BR><TD><CENTER><IMG SRC="image/savwave.jpg"></TR></TABLE><FONT SIZE=4>An accomplished film maker,songwriter <B>Lauren Savoy</B> is a natural story teller.Her economy of style contrasts with Paul's songwriting tactics.  "I have acompletely different songwriting style then Paul.  I love melodyas well, but I'm more naive so I"m forced to keep things simple.  It ended upunlike anything Paul's done before.  No expensive studio's, Frode's raw anddirect drumming, and the live atmosphere of such a basic setup, all added upto a completely original album.<BR><BR>Not that he had had much time before to record it.  Along with <B>Mags Furuholmen</B> and <B>Morten Harket</B> Paul was, and is, part of oneof the world's, and certainly Norway's,  biggest bands.  <B> With well over twenty million album sold, eight MTV awards, a Grammy nomination,  14 #1 hits worldwide, and a #1 US single  ("Take On Me" in 1985), <B>a-ha</b> enjoyed an extravagant run throughout the world.  </B>After six best-selling albums, and nearly non-stop touring, the band decided to take a much-needed break in 1994,giving members a chance to catch their breath an recharge their batteries.<BR><BR><TABLE CELLSPACING=0 CELLPADDING=0><TR><TD><CENTER><IMG SRC="image/lauren.jpg" align=middle><br></CENTER><TD><FONT SIZE=4>Boston-born Lauren met Paul while attending film school in London.  Anaccomplished film maker, Lauren has directed scores of music videos,documentary's and shorts.  This allows Savoy the luxury of keeping theirmusic video's personal and within the bands control.  They can experiment anduse images to further push the music in the direction they originallyintended.<BR><BR></TR></TABLE><FONT SIZE=4>Making <B>MARY IS COMING</B> was a watershed event for the band.  " We had to learnhow to use all the equipment," recalls Lauren," But even though sometimessound would leak, the rawness ended up being very effective."  When the albumwas completed, they took it to Warner Bros, who quickly signed them up forworld wide release.  Only one problem, Savoy needed a bassist.  After goingthrough many demo tapes, Frode and Paul went to CBGB's one night and heardbass player, <B>Greg Calvert</B>, performing on stage.  He was with another band,but they sent him a tape of Savoy and he soon joined up.  Savoy is nowrehearsing for a string of U.S. and European showcase dates to come in thewinter and spring.<BR><BR>So how does Paul feel about staring over after ten years in the spotlight?  Not bad, apparently. " I love starting from scratch,"he says, " To begin fresh and build something from a natural staring point is great.  We are definitely ready to roll."With the release of Savoy's sensational debut album,  Paul's fiery sentiment should come as no surprise.  The album may have begun life as basement tapes, but now <B> Savoy's MARY IS COMING </B>has unquestionably risen to the rooftops.<BR><BR><PRE></PRE><P><CENTER><A HREF="menu.HTM"><IMG SRC="IMAGE/B_ARROW.JPG" border=0></A></CENTER></BODY></HTML>